      "text": "シダレヤナギ は、ヤナギ科ヤナギ属の落葉高木。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Salix babylonica」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salix_babylonica",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1374742262,
      "text": "Salix babylonica is a species of willow native to dry areas of northern China, Korea, Mongolia, Japan, and Siberia but cultivated for millennia elsewhere in Asia, being traded along the Silk Road to southwest Asia and Europe."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Salix babylonica」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salix_babylonica#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1374742262,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "Salix babylonica, especially its pendulous-branched (\"weeping\") form, has been introduced into many other areas, including Europe and the southeastern United States, but beyond China, it has not generally been as successfully cultivated as some of its hybrid derivatives, being sensitive to late-spring frosts.  In the more humid climates of much of Europe and eastern North America, it is susceptible to a canker disease, willow anthracnose (Marssonina salicicola), which makes infected trees very short-lived and unsightly.\n\n■ Cultivars\nSalix babylonica (Babylon willow) has many cultivars, including:\n\n'Babylon' (synonym: 'Napoleon') is the most widely grown cultivar of S. babylonica, with its typical weeping branches.\n'Crispa' (synonym: 'Annularis') is a mutant of 'Babylon', with spirally curled leaves.\nVarious cultivars of Salix matsudana (Chinese willow) are now often included within Salix babylonica, treated more broadly, including:\n\n'Pendula' is a weeping tree, with a silvery shine, hardier, and more disease resistant.\n'Tortuosa' is an upright tree with twisted and contorted branches, marketed as corkscrew willow.\nOther weeping willow cultivars are derived from interspecific Salix hybrids, including S. babylonica in their parentage.  The most widely grown weeping willow cultivar is Salix × sepulcralis 'Chrysocoma', with bright yellowish branchlets.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
